// Grade 5 passages (validated by live-patches/grade-journey/validate.mjs). Built by validate.mjs --write.
export const PASSAGES = [{"id":"g5-u1-p01","unit":"G5-U1","pool":"practice","title":"The Slowest Crop","genre":"narrative","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","text":"Mateo had never planted anything in his life, so when his grandmother rented a small plot at the Elm Street community garden, he expected to be bored. Instead, he became impatient. Abuela handed him a packet of carrot seeds and showed him how to press each one gently into the soil with his fingertip. \"Now we wait,\" she said, brushing the dirt from her knees.\n\nMateo checked the plot the very next morning, but nothing had changed. He checked again after school, and the soil looked exactly the same. By the end of the first week, he was certain the seeds were broken. \"Mr. Okafor's tomatoes are already as tall as my knee,\" he complained. \"Our side of the garden looks like an empty parking lot.\"\n\nAbuela only smiled and handed him the watering can. \"Mr. Okafor planted his tomatoes back in April,\" she said. \"Carrots take their time, because they are busy growing in places where you cannot see them.\"\n\nFor two more weeks, Mateo watered the plot every afternoon. Sometimes he grumbled, and once he almost dug up a seed to find out if anything was happening underground. Abuela caught him with his finger in the dirt and shook her head. \"If you keep checking on it, it will never get the chance to grow,\" she warned.\n\nThen, on a cool morning in June, Mateo spotted a row of feathery green sprouts poking out of the soil. They were so small that he had to kneel down to see them clearly. He was surprised by how proud he felt. He had not made them grow, exactly, but he had cared for them every single day.\n\nOver the summer, Mateo learned to pull weeds without disturbing the roots, and he learned that carrots like loose, sandy soil. He even started giving advice to a younger boy who had just planted beans in the next plot.\n\nBy August, the carrot tops were thick and bushy. Abuela told him it was finally time, and Mateo pulled the first carrot from the ground. It was crooked and bright orange and longer than his whole hand. He held it up for Mr. Okafor to see, and the old man clapped and cheered from across the fence.\n\nThat night, Abuela chopped the carrots into a big pot of vegetable soup. Mateo decided it was the best soup he had ever tasted. When she asked if he wanted to plant again next spring, he did not hesitate for a second. \"Yes,\" he said. \"But this time, I'm going to plant them in April.\"","questions":[{"type":"theme","q":"What is the theme of the story?","options":["Good things often take patience and steady care.","Gardens grow best when you check on them often.","Older people always know more than young people do."],"answer":0},{"type":"main","q":"Which is the best summary of the story?","options":["Mateo and Mr. Okafor race to see who can grow the tallest tomato plants in the garden.","Mateo digs up every one of his carrot seeds because he is sure that they are broken.","Mateo plants carrots, waits impatiently, keeps caring for them, and is proud of the harvest."],"answer":2},{"type":"vocab","q":"What does the word impatient mean in the story?","options":["unable to wait calmly","unwilling to work hard","unable to plant seeds"],"answer":0,"evidence":"Instead, he became impatient."},{"type":"inference","q":"Why does Mateo say he will plant the carrots in April next time?","options":["He hopes to have carrots growing sooner.","He wants to plant on the same day as Abuela.","He forgot which month he planted them."],"answer":0}],"grade":5,"words":428,"auto":{"fk":4.8,"decodable":null,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"g5-u1-p02","unit":"G5-U1","pool":"practice","title":"The Dropped Baton","genre":"narrative","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","text":"Priya had practiced the handoff a hundred times. In the four-person relay, she ran the third leg, and her job was simple: take the baton from Keisha, sprint around the curve, and pass it to Dana, the fastest runner on the team. Coach Rivera always said that a relay is won or lost in the handoffs, not in the running.\n\nOn the morning of the district meet, the stands were packed with families waving signs. Priya's stomach felt like it was full of bees. When the starting horn sounded, Lucia exploded off the line and passed cleanly to Keisha. Their team was in first place.\n\nKeisha raced toward Priya with her arm stretched out. Priya started running, reached back, and felt the smooth metal touch her palm. Then, somehow, her fingers closed a moment too late. The baton bounced once on the track and rolled toward the grass.\n\nHer heart pounded so loudly that she could barely hear the crowd. Priya froze for a second before scooping it up and running as hard as she could. She handed it to Dana, who flew down the final stretch, but three teams had already passed them. They finished in fourth place.\n\nAfterward, Priya sat alone at the end of the bench with a towel over her head. She did not want to see anyone's face. She was sure her teammates were angry, and she would not have blamed them.\n\nThen she felt someone sit down beside her. It was Dana, and Lucia and Keisha were right behind her. \"You know what Coach says,\" Dana told her. \"We win together, and we lose together.\" Keisha nudged her shoulder. \"Besides,\" she added, \"I was the one who handed it to you. Maybe I let go too early.\"\n\nPriya pulled the towel off her head. She had expected her teammates to blame her, but instead they were sharing the blame. That made her feel both better and more determined.\n\nFor the next two weeks, the four girls stayed after practice to work on handoffs. They practiced until the pass felt as natural as breathing. Coach Rivera watched from the bleachers and said they finally looked like one team instead of four separate runners. At the next meet, every exchange was smooth, and they crossed the line in second place. When the results were posted, the four of them jumped and hugged as if they had won a gold medal, because in a way, they had.","questions":[{"type":"theme","q":"Which statement best expresses the theme of the story?","options":["Practice is a waste of time before a big race.","The fastest runner should always go last.","A team shares both its wins and its losses."],"answer":2},{"type":"literal","q":"What does Coach Rivera say about winning a relay?","options":["It is won by the fastest runner.","It is won or lost in the handoffs.","It is won by starting off quickly."],"answer":1,"evidence":"Coach Rivera always said that a relay is won or lost in the handoffs, not in the running."},{"type":"why","q":"Why does Priya sit alone with a towel over her head?","options":["She is tired and wants to rest before the next race.","She is hiding from the bright sun on the bench.","She thinks her teammates are angry about her mistake."],"answer":2},{"type":"craft","q":"What does the phrase \"her stomach felt like it was full of bees\" show about Priya?","options":["She is feeling very hungry.","She is feeling very sick.","She is feeling very nervous."],"answer":2}],"grade":5,"words":411,"auto":{"fk":4.3,"decodable":null,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"g5-u1-c01","unit":"G5-U1","pool":"check","title":"Second Chair","genre":"narrative","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","text":"For two years, Jamal had been the first-chair violinist in the Riverside Youth Orchestra. First chair meant he sat closest to the conductor, played the important solos, and stood up to tune the whole orchestra before every concert. He was proud of that seat, and he had worked hard to earn it.\n\nThen, in September, a new girl named Lena joined the orchestra. She had moved from Chicago, and on her first day she played a piece so beautifully that the room went completely silent. A week later, Ms. Tanaka announced the new seating chart. Lena would be first chair, and Jamal would move to second.\n\nJamal smiled and nodded, but inside he felt hollow. On the bus ride home, he stared out the window and replayed every note he had ever missed. That night he told his older brother, Andre, that he might quit. \"If I'm not the best, what's the point?\" he asked.\n\nAndre set down his phone and looked at him. \"Do you play because you like being first,\" he asked, \"or because you like the music?\" Jamal didn't answer, but the question followed him to sleep.\n\nAt the next rehearsal, Jamal noticed something he had missed before. Lena was nervous. Her bow trembled during the solo, and she kept glancing at the sheet music as if it might change. During the break, she sat by herself, rubbing her hands together.\n\nJamal hesitated, then walked over. \"The solo in the second movement is tricky,\" he said. \"I used to count the rests out loud under my breath. It helped me a lot.\" Lena looked up, surprised. \"You're not mad that I took your seat?\" she asked quietly.\n\nJamal thought about Andre's question. \"A little,\" he admitted. \"But you play really well, and the orchestra sounds better with you in it.\"\n\nFrom then on, the two of them practiced the hard passages together after rehearsal. Lena showed Jamal a new way to hold his bow that made his notes ring more clearly. Jamal taught Lena the tricks he had learned for staying calm on stage. Week by week, both of them improved.\n\nAt the winter concert, Lena played the solo without a single mistake. When the audience applauded, she turned and gave Jamal a small nod. He nodded back, and he realized he was smiling for real. Someone else's success had not taken anything away from him. If anything, it had given him a friend and made him a better musician.","questions":[{"type":"theme","q":"What is the theme of the story?","options":["Another person's success does not take away from your own.","It is better to quit than to lose something you earned.","The most talented musician should always play the solos."],"answer":0},{"type":"main","q":"Which is the best summary of the story?","options":["Jamal loses his first-chair seat to Lena, helps her anyway, and they both grow as friends.","Jamal and his brother Andre practice the violin together every night before a big concert.","Jamal quits the orchestra after a new girl named Lena takes his seat as first chair."],"answer":0},{"type":"evidence","q":"Which sentence best supports the idea that Jamal's attitude changes?","options":["He nodded back, and he realized he was smiling for real.","He was proud of that seat, and he had worked hard to earn it.","Then, in September, a new girl named Lena joined the orchestra."],"answer":0,"evidence":"He nodded back, and he realized he was smiling for real."},{"type":"inference","q":"Why does Andre's question matter to Jamal?","options":["It makes him want to join a different youth orchestra.","It makes him decide to practice longer than Lena every day.","It makes him think about why he really plays the violin."],"answer":2},{"type":"vocab","q":"In the story, what does the word hollow mean?","options":["loud and angry","empty and sad","full and happy"],"answer":1,"evidence":"Jamal smiled and nodded, but inside he felt hollow."}],"grade":5,"words":414,"auto":{"fk":4.3,"decodable":null,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"g5-u1-c02","unit":"G5-U1","pool":"check","title":"The Trail That Moved","genre":"narrative","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","text":"Ruth had been planning the hike with her grandmother for months. Nana Joy had grown up near Pine Hollow State Park, and she still had the trail map she used as a teenager. The paper was soft and folded into a small square, with a red line marking the path to Cedar Falls. \"I've walked this trail fifty times,\" Nana Joy said. \"We can't get lost.\"\n\nThe first mile went exactly as the map promised. They crossed a wooden footbridge, passed a meadow full of yellow wildflowers, and climbed a gentle hill. Then the trail simply ended. In front of them was a wide gully of sand and fallen trees, and the path on the other side was gone.\n\nNana Joy unfolded the map and frowned. \"This doesn't make any sense,\" she said. A ranger came walking up the hill behind them and explained that a flood had washed out that section of the trail six years earlier. The park had built a new route that curved around the ridge.\n\nRuth expected her grandmother to be upset. Instead, Nana Joy laughed and folded the map back into her pocket. \"Well,\" she said, \"I suppose the land has changed, even if my map hasn't. Let's learn the new way.\"\n\nThe ranger showed them the blue paint marks, called blazes, that were painted on the trees along the new trail. Ruth took the lead and searched for each blue rectangle. When the blazes were hard to spot, Nana Joy taught her other tricks. They listened for the sound of rushing water, and they followed the creek uphill because waterfalls are always upstream.\n\nThe new trail was steeper and rockier than the old one. Twice they had to stop and catch their breath. But the new route also led them past things Nana Joy had never seen, including a hidden pond where a great blue heron stood perfectly still in the shallows.\n\nAt last, they heard a roar and stepped out onto a flat rock. Cedar Falls poured down in front of them, white and sparkling in the afternoon light. Nana Joy said it looked exactly the same as it had fifty years ago, but the walk there had been completely new.\n\nOn the drive home, Ruth asked if Nana Joy was going to throw the old map away. Her grandmother shook her head. \"I'll keep it to remember the old trail,\" she said. \"But from now on, I'll trust my eyes more than my paper.\" Ruth smiled, because she knew the best part of the day had come from the plan that went wrong.","questions":[{"type":"theme","q":"Which statement best expresses the theme of the story?","options":["Old maps are more useful than new trail signs.","When plans change, being flexible can lead to good things.","Hiking is only fun when everything goes as planned."],"answer":1},{"type":"main","q":"Which is the best summary of the story?","options":["Ruth and Nana Joy find their old trail washed out, learn a new route, and still reach the falls.","Ruth and Nana Joy get lost in the woods and must wait for a ranger to rescue them at night.","Ruth and Nana Joy draw a brand new map of Pine Hollow State Park after a terrible flood."],"answer":0},{"type":"evidence","q":"Which sentence best supports the idea that Nana Joy accepts the change?","options":["Instead, Nana Joy laughed and folded the map back into her pocket.","In front of them was a wide gully of sand and fallen trees, and the path on the other side was gone.","The first mile went exactly as the map promised."],"answer":0,"evidence":"Instead, Nana Joy laughed and folded the map back into her pocket."},{"type":"sequence","q":"What happened right after the ranger explained about the flood?","options":["They crossed a wooden bridge and a meadow.","They saw the falls from a flat rock.","The ranger showed them the blue blazes."],"answer":2,"evidence":"The ranger showed them the blue paint marks, called blazes, that were painted on the trees along the new trail."},{"type":"craft","q":"Why does the author include the hidden pond with the heron?","options":["to show that the park was too dangerous to visit","to show that the new route had its own rewards","to show that Nana Joy knew every part of the park"],"answer":1}],"grade":5,"words":432,"auto":{"fk":4.1,"decodable":null,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"g5-u1-c03","unit":"G5-U1","pool":"check","title":"Bruised Mangoes","genre":"narrative","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","text":"Every Saturday, Ama helped her aunt run a fruit stall at the Greenway Farmers Market. Auntie Efua sold oranges, plums, pineapples, and, best of all, mangoes so sweet that people lined up for them before the market even opened. Ama's job was to stack the fruit in neat pyramids, count change, and smile at every customer.\n\nOne hot morning in July, a woman in a straw hat stopped at the stall. She picked up a mango, turned it over, and asked Ama for a dozen. \"They're for my daughter's birthday party,\" she explained. \"She has been asking for mango cake all week.\"\n\nAma reached for the basket at the back. As she started filling the bag, she noticed that several of the mangoes had soft brown patches on one side. They had been bumped in the truck that morning. From the outside, most people would never notice, but Ama knew the bruised spots would turn mushy by the next day.\n\nShe glanced at Auntie Efua, who was busy with another customer. The woman in the straw hat was already reaching for her wallet. Ama could simply hand over the bag and take the money. Instead, she took a deep breath.\n\n\"I'm sorry,\" Ama said, \"but some of these mangoes are bruised. They might not last until the party. I only have seven good ones left.\"\n\nThe woman lowered her wallet. For a moment, Ama worried that she had ruined the sale. Auntie Efua had overheard, and her eyebrows rose in surprise. Twelve mangoes was a big order, and seven was not enough for a cake.\n\nThen the woman smiled. \"Thank you for telling me,\" she said. \"Most sellers wouldn't have bothered.\" She bought the seven good mangoes, along with a pineapple, and promised to come back the next week.\n\nAfter she left, Ama waited for her aunt to scold her. Instead, Auntie Efua put an arm around her shoulders. \"You lost us five mangoes today,\" she said, \"but you may have gained us a customer for years.\"\n\nAuntie Efua was right. The woman, whose name turned out to be Mrs. Delgado, came back the following Saturday and the one after that. She told her neighbors that the Efua stall was the most honest one at the market. By the end of the summer, the line in front of their table was longer than ever. Ama learned that telling the truth can cost a little at first, but trust is worth far more than a single sale.","questions":[{"type":"theme","q":"What is the theme of the story?","options":["A seller should always keep the customer waiting.","Honesty builds trust that lasts longer than one sale.","Bruised fruit is just as good as fresh fruit."],"answer":1},{"type":"main","q":"Which is the best summary of the story?","options":["Ama hides the bruised mangoes in the bottom of a bag, and Auntie Efua scolds her for lying.","Ama and her aunt bake a big mango cake together for a little girl's birthday party at the market.","Ama tells a customer about bruised mangoes, loses part of a sale, and gains a loyal customer."],"answer":2},{"type":"evidence","q":"Which sentence best shows that Ama's honesty helped the stall?","options":["Ama reached for the basket at the back.","By the end of the summer, the line in front of their table was longer than ever.","Twelve mangoes was a big order, and seven was not enough for a cake."],"answer":1,"evidence":"By the end of the summer, the line in front of their table was longer than ever."},{"type":"why","q":"Why does Ama worry after she tells the woman the truth?","options":["She thinks her aunt will close the stall.","She thinks she may have ruined the sale.","She thinks the woman will take the bruised fruit."],"answer":1},{"type":"literal","q":"Why were some of the mangoes bruised?","options":["They had been bumped in the truck.","They had been left out in the hot sun.","They had been dropped by a customer."],"answer":0,"evidence":"They had been bumped in the truck that morning."}],"grade":5,"words":416,"auto":{"fk":4.6,"decodable":null,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"g5-u2-p01","unit":"G5-U2","pool":"practice","title":"Feet That Stick","genre":"informational","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","text":"A small lizard runs straight up a glass window, stops, and hangs upside down from the ceiling. It has no glue on its feet, no suction cups, and no hooks. So how does a gecko do it? For a long time, nobody really knew.\n\nGeckos are lizards that live in warm places all over the world. Many kinds can climb smooth walls and even walk across ceilings. This skill helps them catch insects and escape from snakes and birds. Not every gecko can climb this way, though. Some kinds, such as the leopard gecko, live on the ground and have no sticky pads at all.\n\nThe secret is hidden on the bottom of the gecko's toes. If you looked at a climbing gecko's foot, you would see rows of thin ridges, a little like the lines on your fingertips. Under a powerful microscope, each ridge turns out to be covered with millions of tiny hairs called setae. Each seta is much thinner than a human hair. At its end, it splits into hundreds of even smaller tips.\n\nThese tips are so small that they can get extremely close to a surface. When two things are that close together, a very weak pull forms between them. One tip on its own pulls with almost no strength. But a gecko has billions of tips touching the wall at the same time, and all those tiny pulls add up. Scientists have figured out that if every hair on a gecko's feet touched a surface at once, the gecko could hold up far more than its own weight.\n\nSticking is only half the trick. A gecko also has to let go, or it would be stuck in one spot forever. To unstick a foot, the gecko curls its toes upward and peels them off, starting at the tip, much the way you might peel a piece of tape off a table. This lets it take quick steps and run up a wall at high speed.\n\nGecko feet also seem to stay clean. As a gecko walks, dirt tends to fall off its toes instead of building up. That means its feet keep working day after day without being washed.\n\nEngineers have been studying gecko feet for years. They want to build materials that grip tightly but come off easily and leave no sticky mess behind. Researchers have already made gecko-inspired pads that can hang heavy objects on a smooth wall and then be peeled away and used again. Some scientists hope that robots with gecko-style grippers could someday climb buildings or grab objects in space, where ordinary glue and suction do not work well.","questions":[{"type":"evidence","q":"Which sentence best supports the idea that many weak pulls make the grip strong?","options":["But a gecko has billions of tips touching the wall at the same time, and all those tiny pulls add up.","To unstick a foot, the gecko curls its toes upward and peels them off, starting at the tip, much the way you might peel a piece of tape off a table.","Some kinds, such as the leopard gecko, live on the ground and have no sticky pads at all."],"answer":0,"evidence":"But a gecko has billions of tips touching the wall at the same time, and all those tiny pulls add up."},{"type":"literal","q":"How does a gecko unstick its foot from a wall?","options":["It curls its toes upward and peels them off.","It squirts water to wash the tips clean.","It pushes down hard and jumps backward."],"answer":0,"evidence":"To unstick a foot, the gecko curls its toes upward and peels them off, starting at the tip, much the way you might peel a piece of tape off a table."},{"type":"vocab","q":"In the passage, what does the word inspired mean in \"gecko-inspired pads\"?","options":["made from real gecko skin","based on ideas from gecko feet","sold only in places with geckos"],"answer":1,"evidence":"Researchers have already made gecko-inspired pads that can hang heavy objects on a smooth wall and then be peeled away and used again."},{"type":"main","q":"What is the main idea of the passage?","options":["Tiny hairs on their toes let geckos grip and climb.","Geckos use glue on their feet to catch insects.","All lizards can climb walls and walk on ceilings."],"answer":0}],"grade":5,"words":442,"auto":{"fk":5.2,"decodable":null,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"g5-u2-p02","unit":"G5-U2","pool":"practice","title":"The Year Without a Summer","genre":"informational","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","text":"In June of 1816, farmers in Vermont woke up to a strange sight. Snow was falling on their fields of young corn. A few weeks later, in July and August, hard frosts destroyed crops across New England. People wrote in their diaries about wearing winter coats in the middle of summer. Across the ocean in Europe, cold rain fell for weeks, and many harvests failed. That year became known as the Year Without a Summer.\n\nFor a long time, nobody understood what had caused the cold. Today, scientists believe the answer lies thousands of miles away, on an island in Indonesia. In April of 1815, a volcano called Mount Tambora erupted. It was the most powerful eruption in recorded history. The blast was heard more than a thousand miles away, and it blew enormous amounts of ash and gas high into the sky. Ash rained down on nearby islands, burying farms and villages under a thick gray blanket.\n\nMost of the heavy ash fell back to the ground within days. However, the eruption also sent sulfur gas high into the atmosphere, where it formed a thin haze of tiny droplets. Winds carried this haze around the entire planet. The droplets reflected some of the sunlight back into space before it could reach the ground. As a result, average temperatures around the world dropped for more than a year.\n\nThe cold weather caused serious problems. Because crops failed, food became scarce, and prices rose quickly. In parts of Europe, families went hungry, and governments struggled to send enough help. Some farmers in New England gave up on their land entirely and moved west, hoping for a better growing season in places like Ohio.\n\nThe gloomy summer also left a mark on art and history. In Switzerland, a young writer named Mary Shelley was stuck indoors during the endless rain with a group of friends. To pass the time, they challenged each other to write spooky stories. Shelley's idea grew into the novel Frankenstein, which is still read today. Some historians also think the shortage of food for horses encouraged a German inventor named Karl Drais to build an early bicycle in 1817.\n\nThe Year Without a Summer reminds scientists that events in one part of the world can affect people everywhere. A single mountain on a faraway island changed the weather for millions of people who had never heard its name.","questions":[{"type":"evidence","q":"Which sentence best explains why temperatures dropped around the world?","options":["Snow was falling on their fields of young corn.","The droplets reflected some of the sunlight back into space before it could reach the ground.","Some farmers in New England gave up on their land entirely and moved west, hoping for a better growing season in places like Ohio."],"answer":1,"evidence":"The droplets reflected some of the sunlight back into space before it could reach the ground."},{"type":"why","q":"Why did food prices rise in 1816?","options":["Crops failed in the cold weather.","Horses ate most of the corn.","Farmers moved to Switzerland."],"answer":0},{"type":"literal","q":"Where is Mount Tambora located?","options":["on a farm in Vermont","on an island in Indonesia","on a mountain in Switzerland"],"answer":1,"evidence":"Today, scientists believe the answer lies thousands of miles away, on an island in Indonesia."},{"type":"author","q":"Why does the author include the story of Mary Shelley?","options":["to prove that writing spooky stories causes bad weather","to explain exactly how volcanoes form on faraway islands","to show that the cold summer also affected art and writing"],"answer":2}],"grade":5,"words":400,"auto":{"fk":6.8,"decodable":null,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"g5-u2-c01","unit":"G5-U2","pool":"check","title":"Roads That Lasted","genre":"informational","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","text":"More than two thousand years ago, the Romans ruled an empire that stretched from Britain to Egypt. To control such a huge area, they needed a way to move soldiers, messages, and goods quickly. Their answer was a network of roads so well built that some of them are still used today.\n\nThe most famous Roman road is the Appian Way. Work on it began in 312 BC, and it eventually connected the city of Rome to the port of Brindisi in southern Italy. Over the following centuries, the Romans built more than 50,000 miles of paved roads. A traveler could walk on stone from Spain all the way to the Middle East.\n\nRoman roads lasted because they were built in layers. First, workers dug a deep trench and filled the bottom with large stones. Next, they added a layer of smaller rocks and gravel mixed with lime, which held everything together. On top of that went a layer of packed sand or fine gravel. Finally, the builders fitted flat paving stones tightly together to form a smooth surface. Each layer spread out the weight of carts and marching feet so the road would not sink.\n\nThe Romans also planned for rain. Most roads were built slightly higher in the middle than at the edges, so water ran off to the sides instead of sitting in puddles. Ditches along the edges carried the water away. Because water is one of the main reasons roads crack and crumble, this simple design helped the roads survive for centuries.\n\nRoman engineers liked to build roads in straight lines whenever possible. Instead of winding around a hill, a road might go right over it. Straight roads were shorter, and they made it easier for armies to move quickly from one part of the empire to another.\n\nAlong the roads, the Romans placed tall stone markers called milestones. Each one showed the distance to the nearest town or back to Rome. Travelers could also stop at rest stations to change horses, eat a meal, or spend the night. Government messengers used these stations to carry news across the empire in a matter of days.\n\nToday, visitors to Italy can still walk on sections of the Appian Way, with its original paving stones worn smooth by centuries of wheels. The roads are a reminder that careful planning and solid building can create something that lasts far longer than the people who made it.","questions":[{"type":"main","q":"What is the main idea of the passage?","options":["Roman roads were carefully built and lasted for centuries.","Roman soldiers walked from Spain all the way to Egypt.","Roman milestones showed travelers the way back to Rome."],"answer":0},{"type":"evidence","q":"Which sentence best supports the idea that the Romans planned for rain?","options":["Roman engineers liked to build roads in straight lines whenever possible.","Each one showed the distance to the nearest town or back to Rome.","Ditches along the edges carried the water away."],"answer":2,"evidence":"Ditches along the edges carried the water away."},{"type":"evidence","q":"Which sentence best explains why the layers kept roads from sinking?","options":["Travelers could also stop at rest stations to change horses, eat a meal, or spend the night.","Work on it began in 312 BC, and it eventually connected the city of Rome to the port of Brindisi in southern Italy.","Each layer spread out the weight of carts and marching feet so the road would not sink."],"answer":2,"evidence":"Each layer spread out the weight of carts and marching feet so the road would not sink."},{"type":"vocab","q":"What are milestones in the passage?","options":["rest stops where horses are changed","stone markers that show distance","flat stones that pave a road"],"answer":1,"evidence":"Along the roads, the Romans placed tall stone markers called milestones."},{"type":"author","q":"Why does the author describe the steps for building a road in order?","options":["to explain how the layers made the roads strong","to compare Roman roads with roads in Egypt","to persuade readers to visit Italy someday"],"answer":0}],"grade":5,"words":407,"auto":{"fk":6.5,"decodable":null,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"g5-u2-c02","unit":"G5-U2","pool":"check","title":"The Toughest Tiny Animal","genre":"informational","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","text":"If you scoop up a clump of damp moss and look at it under a microscope, you might see a chubby creature lumbering along on eight stubby legs. It is a tardigrade, and it may be the toughest animal on Earth. Because of their round shape and slow, clumsy walk, tardigrades are often called water bears.\n\nTardigrades are tiny. Most are about half a millimeter long, which is smaller than the period at the end of this sentence. A German scientist first described them in 1773, and since then researchers have found more than a thousand different kinds. They live almost everywhere, from mountaintops to the deep sea to the moss growing on city sidewalks. Tardigrades eat by piercing plant cells or tiny creatures with sharp mouthparts and sucking out the juices.\n\nWhat makes tardigrades famous is their ability to survive conditions that would destroy almost any other animal. They need a thin layer of water around their bodies to move and eat. When their home dries out, however, they do not simply perish. Instead, a tardigrade pulls in its legs, loses nearly all the water in its body, and curls into a dry little ball called a tun. In this state, its body almost completely stops working.\n\nA tardigrade can stay in its tun state for years. When water returns, it soaks up the moisture, uncurls, and begins walking again within hours. In one famous experiment, scientists revived tardigrades from a sample of moss that had been dried and stored for a long time.\n\nTardigrades in the tun state can also handle extreme temperatures. In laboratory tests, they have survived being cooled to nearly the coldest temperature possible. Some have also survived short periods of heat far above the boiling point of water.\n\nIn 2007, scientists tested tardigrades in the harshest place they could think of: outer space. A European space mission carried dried tardigrades into orbit and exposed them to the vacuum of space for ten days. When the animals returned to Earth and were given water, many of them came back to life. Some even laid eggs that hatched normally.\n\nScientists are now studying tardigrades to learn exactly how they protect their bodies. Researchers have found special proteins that seem to wrap around and shield the animal's cells when it dries out. Someday, these discoveries might help people store medicines without refrigeration. The tiny water bear may turn out to have very big lessons to teach.","questions":[{"type":"main","q":"What is the main idea of the passage?","options":["Tardigrades are the largest and fastest animals living in damp moss.","Tardigrades were first sent into outer space by a German scientist.","Tardigrades are tiny animals that can survive extreme conditions."],"answer":2},{"type":"evidence","q":"Which sentence best supports the idea that tardigrades can survive in space?","options":["When the animals returned to Earth and were given water, many of them came back to life.","Most are about half a millimeter long, which is smaller than the period at the end of this sentence.","Researchers have found special proteins that seem to wrap around and shield the animal's cells when it dries out."],"answer":0,"evidence":"When the animals returned to Earth and were given water, many of them came back to life."},{"type":"evidence","q":"Which sentence best explains why tardigrades are called water bears?","options":["They need a thin layer of water around their bodies to move and eat.","Because of their round shape and slow, clumsy walk, tardigrades are often called water bears.","Most are about half a millimeter long, which is smaller than the period at the end of this sentence."],"answer":1,"evidence":"Because of their round shape and slow, clumsy walk, tardigrades are often called water bears."},{"type":"vocab","q":"What does the word perish mean in the passage?","options":["to grow bigger","to stop living","to start walking"],"answer":1,"evidence":"When their home dries out, however, they do not simply perish."},{"type":"author","q":"Why does the author end by describing medicines?","options":["to explain how tardigrades are kept in laboratories","to warn readers that tardigrades can make people sick","to show how studying tardigrades might help people"],"answer":2}],"grade":5,"words":409,"auto":{"fk":7.6,"decodable":null,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"g5-u2-c03","unit":"G5-U2","pool":"check","title":"The Engineer Who Made a Splash","genre":"informational","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","text":"Lonnie Johnson was always taking things apart. Growing up in Mobile, Alabama, in the 1950s and 1960s, he opened up his sister's toys to see how they worked and built go-karts from old lawnmower engines. His friends called him \"the Professor,\" and his parents encouraged his curiosity even though money was tight.\n\nIn high school, Johnson decided to build a robot. He used parts from junkyards and scrap piles, including a tape recorder that he turned into the robot's memory. He named it Linex. In 1968, he entered Linex in a state science fair held at the University of Alabama. At that time, many schools and events in the South had only recently been opened to Black students, and Johnson was the only Black student in the competition. He won first prize.\n\nJohnson went on to study engineering at Tuskegee University. Later, he worked for the United States Air Force and then for NASA's Jet Propulsion Laboratory in California. There, he helped design power systems for the Galileo spacecraft, which traveled to Jupiter. His job was to make sure the spacecraft would keep running even if something went wrong millions of miles from home.\n\nAt home, Johnson kept inventing. In 1982, he was working on a new kind of cooling system that used water instead of harmful chemicals. One night, he attached a nozzle he had made to his bathroom sink and turned it on. A powerful stream of water blasted across the room. Johnson's first thought was that the stream would make an amazing toy.\n\nTurning that idea into a real product was not easy. Johnson built a handmade model out of plastic pipe and a soda bottle, and he spent years showing it to toy companies. Many of them were not interested. He kept improving the design and kept asking. Finally, in 1989, a company agreed to make it. The water blaster went on sale soon after, and it was renamed the Super Soaker in 1991. It became one of the best-selling toys in the world.\n\nJohnson used the money from his toy to start his own research company. Today, he holds more than one hundred patents, and he continues to work on ideas for cleaner energy. When students ask him for advice, he tells them that failure is part of the process. In his view, an inventor is simply someone who refuses to stop trying.","questions":[{"type":"main","q":"Which is the best summary of the passage?","options":["Lonnie Johnson invented a cooling system, sold it to NASA, and then retired from inventing for good.","Lonnie Johnson grew up in California and built spacecraft that traveled all the way to the planet Mars.","Lonnie Johnson built a robot, worked for NASA, and kept trying until his toy idea became a success."],"answer":2},{"type":"evidence","q":"Which sentence best supports that Johnson was persistent?","options":["He kept improving the design and kept asking.","He named it Linex.","Johnson went on to study engineering at Tuskegee University."],"answer":0,"evidence":"He kept improving the design and kept asking."},{"type":"evidence","q":"Which sentence best shows that Johnson was curious as a child?","options":["His friends called him \"the Professor,\" and his parents encouraged his curiosity even though money was tight.","Later, he worked for the United States Air Force and then for NASA's Jet Propulsion Laboratory in California.","He won first prize."],"answer":0,"evidence":"His friends called him \"the Professor,\" and his parents encouraged his curiosity even though money was tight."},{"type":"sequence","q":"What happened after Johnson attached a nozzle to his sink?","options":["He won first prize at a state science fair in Alabama.","He helped design power systems for the Galileo spacecraft.","A powerful stream of water blasted across the room."],"answer":2,"evidence":"A powerful stream of water blasted across the room."},{"type":"inference","q":"What does the passage suggest about how inventions succeed?","options":["They usually succeed on the very first try.","They usually need hard work and patience.","They usually come from large toy companies."],"answer":1}],"grade":5,"words":400,"auto":{"fk":6.8,"decodable":null,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"g5-u3-p01","unit":"G5-U3","pool":"practice","title":"Trees in the City","genre":"informational","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","text":"## Text 1: Why Cities Need Trees\n\nOn a hot summer afternoon, a city can feel like an oven. Streets, sidewalks, and rooftops soak up heat from the sun all day and release it slowly at night. Scientists call this the urban heat island effect, and it can make a city several degrees warmer than the countryside around it.\n\nTrees are one of the best ways to reduce that heat. Their leaves block sunlight before it reaches the ground, and shaded sidewalks can be more than twenty degrees cooler than sunny ones. Trees also release water vapor through their leaves, which cools the air around them, a little like sweat cooling your skin.\n\nTrees help in other ways, too. Their roots soak up rainwater that would otherwise rush into storm drains and cause flooding. A single large tree can take in hundreds of gallons of rain in a year. Leaves also trap dust and help clean the air. Because of these benefits, many cities have started programs to plant thousands of new trees along their streets.\n\nTrees can even save money. When homes and buildings are shaded, people do not need to run their air conditioners as often. Studies have also found that people enjoy walking more on streets lined with trees, and shoppers tend to stay longer in leafy business districts.\n\n## Text 2: A Day with the Tree Crew\n\nLast Saturday, I joined my neighborhood's volunteer tree crew. Our goal was to plant twelve young trees along Bell Street, which has almost no shade at all. My neighbor Mr. Haddad, who leads the crew, said that our block gets hotter than any other block in the neighborhood.\n\nWe started at eight in the morning, before the heat set in. Each tree came in a big plastic pot, and the city had already cut square holes in the sidewalk for them. My job was to loosen the roots with my fingers so they would spread out in the soil. Then two adults lowered each tree into its hole, and we filled the space with dirt and mulch.\n\nBy noon, my arms ached and my shirt was soaked. But when I looked down Bell Street, I could picture how it would look in ten years, with leafy branches reaching over the sidewalk. Mr. Haddad told us that the trees will need water every week for their first two summers. I signed up to water the maple in front of my building. I named it Rosie, and I plan to sit in its shade someday.","questions":[{"type":"compare","q":"What idea do both texts share?","options":["Trees should only be planted in the country.","Trees can help make city streets cooler.","Trees need no care after they are planted."],"answer":1},{"type":"literal","q":"According to Text 1, how do tree roots help a city?","options":["They soak up rainwater that would cause flooding.","They break up sidewalks so grass can grow.","They store heat from the sun during the day."],"answer":0,"evidence":"Their roots soak up rainwater that would otherwise rush into storm drains and cause flooding."},{"type":"compare","q":"How is Text 2 different from Text 1?","options":["Text 2 explains how leaves trap dust in the air.","Text 2 gives facts about the urban heat island effect.","Text 2 tells one person's experience planting trees."],"answer":2},{"type":"inference","q":"Why does the crew plant trees along Bell Street?","options":["The city wants to cut down the old trees there.","The street already has more trees than any other.","The street has almost no shade and gets very hot."],"answer":2}],"grade":5,"words":422,"auto":{"fk":5.5,"decodable":null,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"g5-u3-p02","unit":"G5-U3","pool":"practice","title":"Reading With Fingertips","genre":"informational","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","text":"## Text 1: The Boy Who Invented an Alphabet of Dots\n\nLouis Braille was born in 1809 in a small village near Paris, France. His father made leather harnesses for horses. When Louis was three years old, he was playing with a sharp tool in his father's workshop and hurt one of his eyes. An infection spread to his other eye, and by the age of five, he was completely blind.\n\nLouis was a bright student. At ten, he went to a special school for blind children in Paris. The school had a few books printed with large raised letters that students could feel with their fingers. These books were huge and heavy, and reading them was very slow. Writing that way was nearly impossible.\n\nThen a visitor named Charles Barbier came to the school. He had invented a code of raised dots that soldiers could read in the dark. His system was hard to learn, but it gave Louis an idea. Louis worked on it for years. By the time he was fifteen, he had created a much simpler code. Each letter was made from up to six dots in a small cell, two dots wide and three dots tall. A single fingertip could feel a whole letter at once.\n\nLouis later became a teacher at the same school. His code, now called braille, is used for reading and writing in many languages around the world.\n\n## Text 2: My First Braille Book\n\nWhen I was seven, my eyesight started getting worse. Doctors told my family that I would need to learn braille. At first I was upset. I wanted to read books the same way my friends did.\n\nMy teacher, Mr. Okafor, started me with just a few letters. I practiced feeling the dots with the pads of my fingers, moving them slowly from left to right across the page. The letter A is one dot. The letter B is two dots, one above the other. Some letters felt almost the same, and I mixed them up all the time.\n\nAfter a few months, I could read short words. After a year, I read my first chapter book all by myself. I remember sitting on my bed and laughing at a funny part, and my mom ran in to see what was so funny.\n\nNow I notice braille everywhere. It is on elevator buttons, on signs outside classroom doors, and on some menus at restaurants. Every time I find it, I think about a boy who was not much older than me when he invented it.","questions":[{"type":"compare","q":"How does Text 2 add to what readers learn in Text 1?","options":["It shows what learning braille is like for one student today.","It explains how Charles Barbier invented his code for soldiers.","It describes the heavy books with large raised letters in Paris."],"answer":0},{"type":"literal","q":"According to Text 1, how many dots can make up one braille letter?","options":["up to six dots in a small cell","up to twelve dots in a long row","up to three dots in a circle"],"answer":0,"evidence":"Each letter was made from up to six dots in a small cell, two dots wide and three dots tall."},{"type":"inference","q":"Using both texts, why does braille work well for readers?","options":["Readers feel each letter with one fingertip.","Readers can see the dots in very dim light.","Readers need only a few very large books."],"answer":0},{"type":"main","q":"What is Text 1 mostly about?","options":["how Louis Braille created a reading code for blind people","how soldiers read secret messages at night during battles","how harnesses for horses were made in a small French village"],"answer":0}],"grade":5,"words":426,"auto":{"fk":4.3,"decodable":null,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"g5-u3-c01","unit":"G5-U3","pool":"check","title":"Taking to the Sky","genre":"informational","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","text":"## Text 1: The Brothers from Dayton\n\nWilbur and Orville Wright never finished college, but they became two of the most important inventors in history. In the 1890s, the brothers ran a bicycle shop in Dayton, Ohio. Building bicycles taught them about balance, light frames, and careful design. They became fascinated by a question that many people thought was impossible to answer: could a person fly in a machine that was heavier than air?\n\nThe Wrights studied birds and read everything they could find about flying. They built gliders and tested them on the windy sand dunes of the North Carolina coast. When their early gliders did not lift as well as they expected, they built their own wind tunnel and tested more than two hundred different wing shapes. Their experiments taught them how to control an aircraft by bending the tips of its wings.\n\nOn December 17, 1903, Orville lay on the lower wing of their engine-powered Flyer and took off into a cold wind. The flight lasted only twelve seconds and covered about 120 feet. Later that day, Wilbur flew for almost a minute. The age of the airplane had begun. The brothers kept improving their design, and by 1905 their new Flyer could circle in the air for more than half an hour.\n\n## Text 2: Queen Bess\n\nBessie Coleman was born in Texas in 1892, one of thirteen children. As a young woman, she moved to Chicago and worked in a barbershop, where she heard stories from soldiers who had flown airplanes in Europe. She decided that she wanted to fly too.\n\nThere was one big problem. In the United States at that time, flight schools would not accept a Black woman as a student. Coleman refused to give up. She learned French, saved her money, and traveled to France, where a flight school welcomed her. A newspaper publisher in Chicago named Robert Abbott helped her raise money for the trip. In 1921, she became the first African American woman to earn a pilot's license.\n\nBack in America, Coleman became a star of air shows. Crowds gasped as she flew loops, figure eights, and daring dives. Newspapers called her \"Queen Bess.\" She used her fame to speak at schools and churches, urging young Black Americans to follow their dreams. She also refused to perform at shows where Black visitors had to enter through a separate gate from white visitors. She hoped to open a flight school of her own so that others would not face the barriers she had faced.","questions":[{"type":"compare","q":"What do the Wright brothers and Bessie Coleman have in common?","options":["They both learned how to fly airplanes at a famous flight school in France.","They both worked hard to reach a goal others thought was out of reach.","They both built their own wind tunnels to test many different wing shapes."],"answer":1},{"type":"evidence","q":"Which sentence from Text 2 best shows that Coleman faced unfair barriers?","options":["In the United States at that time, flight schools would not accept a Black woman as a student.","As a young woman, she moved to Chicago and worked in a barbershop, where she heard stories from soldiers who had flown airplanes in Europe.","Crowds gasped as she flew loops, figure eights, and daring dives."],"answer":0,"evidence":"In the United States at that time, flight schools would not accept a Black woman as a student."},{"type":"literal","q":"According to Text 1, how long did Orville's first flight last?","options":["almost a minute","about two hours","twelve seconds"],"answer":2,"evidence":"The flight lasted only twelve seconds and covered about 120 feet."},{"type":"compare","q":"How is the main challenge in Text 1 different from the one in Text 2?","options":["The Wrights had to invent a working airplane, but Coleman had to find a school that would teach her.","The Wrights had to find a teacher in France, but Coleman had to build a wind tunnel at home.","The Wrights had to learn French to fly, but Coleman had to learn how to build bicycles in Ohio."],"answer":0},{"type":"main","q":"What topic do both texts help explain?","options":["the history of bicycle shops","the history of air shows in France","the early history of flying"],"answer":2}],"grade":5,"words":423,"auto":{"fk":6.4,"decodable":null,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"g5-u3-c02","unit":"G5-U3","pool":"check","title":"Racing Through the Snow","genre":"informational","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","text":"## Text 1: The Serum Run of 1925\n\nIn January 1925, a doctor in Nome, Alaska, faced a terrible problem. Several children in the town had come down with diphtheria, a serious disease that spreads easily and makes it hard to breathe. The doctor, Curtis Welch, knew that a medicine called antitoxin could stop it. But his supply was old, and the nearest fresh medicine was in Anchorage, nearly a thousand miles away.\n\nIt was the middle of winter. The sea around Nome was frozen, so ships could not reach it, and airplanes of the time could not fly safely in such cold. Leaders decided to send the medicine by train as far as the town of Nenana. From there, teams of sled dogs would carry it the rest of the way, about 674 miles across snow and ice.\n\nAbout twenty mushers and more than one hundred fifty dogs took part. Each team raced its part of the trail and then handed the package to the next team, like runners in a relay. Temperatures dropped far below zero, and strong winds blew snow so hard that drivers sometimes could not see their own dogs. Musher Leonhard Seppala and his lead dog, Togo, ran the longest and most dangerous stretch, including a crossing of a frozen bay where the ice could break apart. Gunnar Kaasen and his lead dog, Balto, carried the medicine on the final leg into Nome.\n\nThe medicine arrived on February 2, after about five and a half days on the trail. The whole country had followed the news, and the dogs became heroes. Today a statue of Balto stands in Central Park in New York City.\n\n## Text 2: Watching the Iditarod\n\nLast March, my family flew to Anchorage to watch the start of the Iditarod, a sled dog race of about a thousand miles to Nome. The race began in 1973, and it honors the old trail and the teams that once carried mail and supplies across Alaska.\n\nI was surprised by how excited the dogs were. They barked, jumped, and pulled at their lines, eager to run. Each musher had a team of up to fourteen dogs. Volunteers held the sleds back until the countdown finished.\n\nBefore the race, a musher named Ana showed me the tiny fabric booties that protect her dogs' paws from sharp ice. She said that she stops many times to rest, feed the dogs, and check every paw. \"The dogs are athletes,\" she told me. \"My most important job is taking care of them.\"\n\nThe race usually takes more than a week. I followed Ana on a tracking website every night until she crossed the finish line in Nome.","questions":[{"type":"compare","q":"How are the dog teams in the two texts alike?","options":["Both travel long distances across snowy Alaska to Nome.","Both carry medicine to sick children who need help.","Both race on a trail that starts at the town of Nenana."],"answer":0},{"type":"evidence","q":"Which sentence from Text 1 best explains why sled dogs were needed?","options":["Musher Leonhard Seppala and his lead dog, Togo, ran the longest and most dangerous stretch, including a crossing of a frozen bay where the ice could break apart.","The sea around Nome was frozen, so ships could not reach it, and airplanes of the time could not fly safely in such cold.","The medicine arrived on February 2, after about five and a half days on the trail."],"answer":1,"evidence":"The sea around Nome was frozen, so ships could not reach it, and airplanes of the time could not fly safely in such cold."},{"type":"literal","q":"According to Text 1, who ran the final leg into Nome?","options":["Gunnar Kaasen and his lead dog, Balto","Leonhard Seppala and his lead dog, Togo","Curtis Welch and a team from Anchorage"],"answer":0,"evidence":"Gunnar Kaasen and his lead dog, Balto, carried the medicine on the final leg into Nome."},{"type":"inference","q":"Why does Ana stop many times during the race?","options":["to keep her dogs healthy enough to finish","to wait for the medicine to reach her sled","to let the volunteers hold her sled back"],"answer":0},{"type":"compare","q":"What does Text 1 explain that Text 2 leaves out?","options":["why the long trail to Nome first became famous","what dog booties are used for on the sharp ice","how long the race usually takes to finish today"],"answer":0}],"grade":5,"words":447,"auto":{"fk":6.4,"decodable":null,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"g5-u3-c03","unit":"G5-U3","pool":"check","title":"Lights on the Shore","genre":"informational","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","text":"## Text 1: How a Lighthouse Works\n\nFor thousands of years, sailors have feared rocky coasts at night. A ship that cannot see the shore may crash into hidden rocks. Lighthouses were built to warn them. One of the most famous was the Pharos of Alexandria in Egypt, built more than two thousand years ago. Its keepers burned fires at the top of a tall tower.\n\nEarly lighthouses used wood fires, then candles, and later oil lamps with shiny metal mirrors behind them. Even so, much of the light escaped in every direction, and the beams were often too weak to see from far away.\n\nIn 1822, a French scientist named Augustin Fresnel invented a new kind of lens. Instead of one thick piece of glass, his lens was made of many rings of glass prisms arranged around the lamp. The prisms bent the light and gathered it into one powerful beam. On a clear night, a light with a Fresnel lens could be seen more than twenty miles out at sea.\n\nSailors also need to know which lighthouse they are looking at. That is why each one has its own pattern of light. One might flash every five seconds, while another shines steadily or flashes red. In the daytime, lighthouses are recognized by the colors and stripes painted on their towers, which are called daymarks. Today most lighthouses run automatically, and ships also use satellites to find their way, but many lights still shine every night.\n\n## Text 2: Climbing Cape Hatteras\n\nLast summer my family visited the Cape Hatteras Lighthouse on the coast of North Carolina. It is the tallest brick lighthouse in the United States, and its tower is painted with a black and white stripe that spirals all the way to the top.\n\nA park ranger told us that the ocean had been washing away the beach in front of the lighthouse for many years. By the 1990s, the waves were getting dangerously close. So in 1999, workers lifted the whole tower onto steel tracks and slowly rolled it about half a mile inland. It took weeks to move, and the lighthouse never cracked.\n\nThen we climbed more than two hundred fifty steps to the top. My legs were burning by the end, but the view was worth it. I could see the ocean stretching out on one side and the sound on the other. My little sister counted ships on the water. Now I understand why sailors were so glad to see a light like this one.","questions":[{"type":"compare","q":"How does the stripe in Text 2 connect to an idea in Text 1?","options":["It is a daymark that helps sailors know the lighthouse.","It is a lens that bends light into one strong beam.","It is a mirror that keeps the lamp from going out."],"answer":0},{"type":"evidence","q":"Which sentence from Text 1 best explains why the Fresnel lens worked so well?","options":["Its keepers burned fires at the top of a tall tower.","The prisms bent the light and gathered it into one powerful beam.","One might flash every five seconds, while another shines steadily or flashes red."],"answer":1,"evidence":"The prisms bent the light and gathered it into one powerful beam."},{"type":"literal","q":"According to Text 2, what did workers do to the lighthouse in 1999?","options":["They rolled it about half a mile inland.","They painted it with new red stripes.","They added a new lens with more prisms."],"answer":0,"evidence":"So in 1999, workers lifted the whole tower onto steel tracks and slowly rolled it about half a mile inland."},{"type":"inference","q":"Why was the lighthouse in Text 2 moved?","options":["The ocean might soon have reached and harmed it.","The tower was too short for ships to see it.","Visitors wanted a better view of the sound."],"answer":0},{"type":"main","q":"What main idea do both texts share?","options":["Lighthouses are important and people work to keep them.","Lighthouses are no longer useful to ships of any kind.","Lighthouses were first invented in North Carolina."],"answer":0}],"grade":5,"words":420,"auto":{"fk":5.8,"decodable":null,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"g5-book-ch1","unit":"G5-U4","pool":"book","title":"The Box in the Attic","genre":"narrative","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","text":"Kenji Mori had been at his grandmother's house for exactly two days, and he was already counting the hours until he could go home. Grandma Hiroko lived alone at the top of Juniper Hill, a dusty ridge in eastern Oregon where the nearest store was twenty minutes away. Her house had no internet, and his phone showed a single weak bar of signal only if he stood on the back porch and held it over his head.\n\nHis cousin Rosa, who was also spending the summer there, did not seem to mind. Rosa was a year older than Kenji and could happily read for six hours straight. \"You could try a book,\" she suggested, without looking up from hers. Kenji groaned and flopped onto the couch.\n\nOn the third morning, a thunderstorm rolled over the hill, and rain drummed on the roof. Grandma Hiroko handed them two flashlights. \"If you're going to be restless,\" she said, \"be restless somewhere useful. The attic needs sorting.\"\n\nThe attic was hot and smelled like old cardboard. Rosa sorted boxes of photographs while Kenji poked through the corners. Behind a stack of winter blankets, he found a heavy metal case with rows of black knobs, a round dial covered in numbers, and a microphone on a curled cord. A layer of dust covered everything, so he wiped the front with his sleeve.\n\n\"Rosa,\" he called. \"What is this thing?\"\n\nRosa came over and knelt beside him. \"I think it's a radio,\" she said slowly, \"but not the kind you use to listen to music.\" Tucked beside the case was a thin notebook with a cracked green cover. Inside, page after page was filled with neat handwriting in blue ink. Each line listed a date, a time, a string of letters and numbers, and a place: Japan, Brazil, Kenya, Chile, Canada.\n\n\"These are conversations,\" Rosa said. \"Someone was talking to people all over the world.\"\n\nKenji turned to the inside cover. Written there in capital letters was a name he recognized: HIROKO MORI.\n\nThey carried the radio and the notebook downstairs. Grandma Hiroko was chopping onions at the kitchen counter, and when she saw what they were holding, her hands stopped in midair. For a long moment, she did not say anything. Then she wiped her hands on a towel and touched the cover of the notebook very gently, the way someone might touch an old friend's face.\n\n\"That old thing hasn't worked in thirty years,\" she said quietly. \"I'd almost forgotten it was up there.\"\n\n\"Did you really talk to people in Kenya?\" Kenji asked. \"And Chile?\"\n\nHis grandmother smiled, but her eyes looked far away. \"I did,\" she said. \"Every Sunday night for a long time.\" Then she turned back to her onions, and Kenji could tell that the conversation was over, at least for now.\n\nThat night, Kenji lay awake listening to the rain. He kept thinking about the knobs, the dial, and the blue handwriting. For the first time since he arrived, he was not bored at all. He had found a mystery, and he wanted to solve it.","book":"The Radio on Juniper Hill","chapter":1,"questions":[{"type":"why","q":"Why is Kenji unhappy at the start of the chapter?","options":["He does not like spending time with Rosa.","He is afraid of the storms on Juniper Hill.","He misses having internet and things to do."],"answer":2},{"type":"literal","q":"What name did Kenji find written inside the notebook's cover?","options":["Hiroko Mori","Kenta Mori","Kenji Mori"],"answer":0,"evidence":"Written there in capital letters was a name he recognized: HIROKO MORI."},{"type":"inference","q":"What does Grandma Hiroko's reaction suggest about the radio?","options":["It brings back strong memories for her.","She is angry that the children went upstairs.","She has never seen the radio before."],"answer":0},{"type":"craft","q":"Why does the author end the chapter by calling the radio a mystery?","options":["to show how Kenji's feelings have changed","to show that Rosa wants to go back home","to explain how old radios used to work"],"answer":0}],"grade":5,"words":518,"auto":{"fk":5.1,"decodable":null,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"g5-book-ch2","unit":"G5-U4","pool":"book","title":"Wires Between the Trees","genre":"narrative","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","text":"The next morning, Kenji asked his grandmother if they could try to fix the radio. She hesitated, then shrugged. \"You can try,\" she said. \"But you'll need help from someone who knows more than I remember. Go see Mrs. Okonkwo at the bottom of the hill.\"\n\nMrs. Okonkwo turned out to be a retired electrician with silver braids and a workshop full of tools. When Kenji and Rosa set the radio on her workbench, she let out a low whistle. \"This is a beauty,\" she said. \"Your grandmother used to be famous around here, you know. Folks called her the Voice of Juniper Hill.\"\n\nMrs. Okonkwo explained that the radio was a ham radio, a kind of two-way radio that people use to talk with each other over long distances. \"Anyone can listen,\" she said, \"but to transmit, which means to send your own voice out, you need a license from the government. You have to pass a test to get one.\" She tapped the case. \"I have a license, so I can help you test it.\"\n\nFor the rest of the week, Kenji and Rosa spent every afternoon in the workshop. Mrs. Okonkwo showed them how to use a meter to find the parts that were not working. They discovered a burned-out fuse and a cracked wire inside the power cord. Kenji's hands shook the first time he used a soldering iron, but Mrs. Okonkwo guided him patiently. \"Slow is smooth,\" she told him, \"and smooth is fast.\"\n\nNext, they needed an antenna. Rosa read through an old manual and figured out that a long wire would work if it was stretched high enough. On Saturday, the three of them climbed Juniper Hill with a coil of copper wire and a ladder. They tied one end to a tall juniper tree near the house and the other end to a second tree across the yard. The wire hung in the air like a thin silver line drawn across the sky.\n\nThat evening, Kenji plugged in the radio and turned the largest knob. At first there was only a soft hiss. Then, as the dial moved, a burst of quick beeps came through the speaker, followed by a man's voice speaking Spanish. Rosa grabbed Kenji's arm. Mrs. Okonkwo checked the dial and grinned. \"That sounds like it's coming from somewhere in South America,\" she said.\n\nBehind them, the screen door creaked. Grandma Hiroko stood in the doorway, holding a dish towel. She stepped closer and listened, and slowly a smile spread across her face.\n\n\"Those beeps are Morse code,\" she said. \"Each letter is a pattern of short and long sounds.\" She closed her eyes and tapped her fingers on the table in time with the signal. \"He's saying hello to anyone who can hear him.\"\n\nThen Grandma Hiroko sat down and began to talk. She told them that she had started using the radio when she was fifteen. Back then, it was the only way a girl on a lonely hill could make friends across oceans. Her favorite contact had been a boy named Tomás in Chile, who practiced English with her while she practiced Spanish. They had talked every Sunday for almost ten years.\n\n\"Why did you stop?\" Rosa asked softly.\n\nGrandma Hiroko looked at the glowing dial for a long time. \"Life got busy,\" she said at last. \"I always meant to come back to it.\"","book":"The Radio on Juniper Hill","chapter":2,"questions":[{"type":"vocab","q":"What does the word transmit mean in the chapter?","options":["to send your voice out","to fix a broken wire","to listen without talking"],"answer":0,"evidence":"\"Anyone can listen,\" she said, \"but to transmit, which means to send your own voice out, you need a license from the government."},{"type":"sequence","q":"What did the children do with the copper wire after fixing the fuse?","options":["They found the radio in the attic.","They read the notebook for the first time.","They tied one end to a juniper tree."],"answer":2,"evidence":"They tied one end to a tall juniper tree near the house and the other end to a second tree across the yard."},{"type":"evidence","q":"Which sentence best shows that Grandma Hiroko is happy to hear the radio again?","options":["She stepped closer and listened, and slowly a smile spread across her face.","Behind them, the screen door creaked.","Rosa read through an old manual and figured out that a long wire would work if it was stretched high enough."],"answer":0,"evidence":"She stepped closer and listened, and slowly a smile spread across her face."},{"type":"inference","q":"Why did the radio matter so much to Grandma Hiroko as a teenager?","options":["It helped her make friends far away from her lonely hill.","It helped her earn money as a famous radio singer.","It helped her study to become an electrician."],"answer":0}],"grade":5,"words":571,"auto":{"fk":3.8,"decodable":null,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"g5-book-ch3","unit":"G5-U4","pool":"book","title":"The Night the Lights Went Out","genre":"narrative","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","text":"For the next three weeks, Grandma Hiroko spent her evenings at the kitchen table with a thick study guide. Her old license had expired long ago, and to get a new one she had to take the test again. Kenji and Rosa quizzed her with flash cards about antennas, radio waves, and safety rules. When she passed the exam on a Tuesday morning, Rosa baked a lopsided chocolate cake to celebrate.\n\nFour days later, the wind arrived. It started as a low moan in the afternoon and grew into a roar by dinnertime. The juniper trees bent sideways, and branches skittered across the yard like tumbleweeds. Just after seven o'clock, the lights flickered twice and went out. Kenji checked his phone, but even the single bar of signal had disappeared.\n\nThey were lighting candles when headlights swung into the driveway. Their neighbor, Mr. Begay, hurried to the porch with rain dripping from his hat. \"A big tree came down across Hill Road,\" he said, out of breath. \"It pulled the power lines down with it. The wires are lying right across the road, and nobody in town knows about it yet.\"\n\nGrandma Hiroko's face became very calm and serious. \"My daughter-in-law drives home from her shift at the clinic on that road around nine,\" Mr. Begay added. \"If she comes around that curve in the dark, she won't see the wires until it's too late.\"\n\nKenji looked at the radio sitting on the side table. \"Grandma,\" he said, \"can you call for help?\"\n\nHis grandmother was already moving. \"Kenji, bring the car battery from the garage, the one Mrs. Okonkwo showed you how to connect. Rosa, get the green notebook and a pen.\" Within ten minutes, the radio was glowing in the candlelight, powered by the battery on the floor.\n\nGrandma Hiroko turned the dial to a frequency that the county's volunteer radio operators used during emergencies. She pressed the button on the microphone and spoke clearly. \"This is Hiroko Mori on Juniper Hill. I have an emergency report. Power lines are down across Hill Road near mile marker four. Please notify the power company and the sheriff.\"\n\nFor a few seconds, there was only static. Kenji held his breath. Then a woman's voice crackled through the speaker. \"Juniper Hill, this is county emergency net. We copy your report. Power lines down on Hill Road at mile four. Passing it along now.\"\n\nMr. Begay asked if the operator could also reach the clinic. Grandma Hiroko relayed the message, and twenty minutes later the voice returned to say that Mr. Begay's daughter-in-law had been told to stay in town for the night. Their neighbor let out a long breath and sank into a chair.\n\nWhile the wind howled outside, Rosa wrote every message in the notebook, just as Grandma had done decades before. Kenji kept the flashlight pointed at the dial so his grandmother could see. Every so often, another voice would report a fallen tree or a flooded road from somewhere across the county, and the operators would pass the news along, one person to the next.\n\nKenji realized that the radio was more than an old machine. On a night when phones and lights had failed, it had connected people who needed each other. He watched his grandmother's steady hand on the microphone and decided that someday, he wanted to be able to do the same thing.","book":"The Radio on Juniper Hill","chapter":3,"questions":[{"type":"literal","q":"Why did Grandma Hiroko have to take the radio test again?","options":["Her old radio had broken in the storm.","Her old license had expired long ago.","Her old notebook had been lost in the attic."],"answer":1,"evidence":"Her old license had expired long ago, and to get a new one she had to take the test again."},{"type":"evidence","q":"Which sentence best shows that the radio message helped Mr. Begay's family?","options":["They were lighting candles when headlights swung into the driveway.","Kenji checked his phone, but even the single bar of signal had disappeared.","Their neighbor let out a long breath and sank into a chair."],"answer":2,"evidence":"Their neighbor let out a long breath and sank into a chair."},{"type":"inference","q":"Why did the radio still work after the power went out?","options":["It was powered by a car battery.","It was plugged into Mr. Begay's truck.","It was charged by the strong wind."],"answer":0},{"type":"craft","q":"How does the author build tension when Grandma first calls for help?","options":["by describing a pause of static while Kenji holds his breath","by describing Rosa baking a lopsided chocolate cake","by describing the flash cards about antennas and waves"],"answer":0}],"grade":5,"words":572,"auto":{"fk":5.5,"decodable":null,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"g5-book-ch4","unit":"G5-U4","pool":"book","title":"Sunday Night","genre":"narrative","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","text":"By the next afternoon, the wind had calmed, and a county crew was cutting the fallen tree into pieces with chainsaws. The power came back on just before dinner. A few days later, the local newspaper ran a short story on its front page with a photo of Grandma Hiroko standing beside her radio. The headline read, \"The Voice of Juniper Hill Returns.\"\n\nGrandma Hiroko pretended to be embarrassed, but Kenji noticed that she bought three copies. She taped one to the refrigerator, right beside Rosa's drawing of the antenna strung between the trees.\n\nAfter the storm, something in the house had changed. Every evening, Grandma Hiroko sat down at the radio and talked with operators from Alaska, Mexico, and even Japan. Sometimes Kenji and Rosa joined her. Because Grandma was a licensed operator, she explained, the children could speak into the microphone as long as she was right there supervising. Kenji loved to ask people what the weather was like where they lived. Rosa kept the log, and her handwriting began to fill the pages that had been blank for thirty years.\n\nOn their last Sunday night on Juniper Hill, Kenji had an idea. \"Grandma,\" he said, \"what if we tried to reach Chile?\" His grandmother laughed and said that Tomás was probably not on the radio anymore. Still, she turned the dial and called out the old letters and numbers she found in the green notebook.\n\nFor a long time, nobody answered. Kenji was about to give up when a young woman's voice came through the speaker, speaking careful English. Her name was Valentina, and she was calling from a town near Santiago. The station belonged to her grandfather, she explained. He had taught her to use it when she was a child.\n\nGrandma Hiroko gripped the edge of the table. \"Is your grandfather Tomás?\" she asked.\n\nThere was a pause. Then an older man's voice came on the air, slow and warm. \"Hiroko,\" he said. \"Is that really you?\"\n\nFor the next hour, Grandma Hiroko and Tomás talked in a mix of English and Spanish, laughing at stories that Kenji and Rosa did not completely understand. They talked about their children, their gardens, and the people they had met on the radio long ago. Tears ran down Grandma's cheeks, but she was smiling the whole time. Before they signed off, Tomás and Grandma promised to talk again the following Sunday, just like they used to.\n\nThe next morning, Kenji's father arrived to drive him home. As they rolled down Hill Road, Kenji's phone buzzed again and again as it found a signal, filling up with messages and videos he had missed all summer. He glanced at the screen and then set the phone face down on the seat.\n\nOn his lap was a study guide that Mrs. Okonkwo had given him for the beginner's radio license. He had already read the first two chapters. Kenji thought about the wire between the juniper trees, the voices in the storm, and the moment two old friends found each other again across thousands of miles. Some connections, he decided, are worth waiting for.","book":"The Radio on Juniper Hill","chapter":4,"questions":[{"type":"theme","q":"Which statement best expresses a theme of the whole novel?","options":["Real connections with people can last across time and distance.","Old machines should be thrown away when new ones are made.","It is better to stay home than to visit faraway places."],"answer":0},{"type":"main","q":"Which is the best summary of the whole novel?","options":["Kenji spends the summer reading books with Rosa, bakes a cake, and wins a prize for drawing an antenna between trees.","Kenji finds a ham radio, helps fix it, sees it save a family in a storm, and reunites his grandmother with an old friend.","Kenji travels to Chile with his grandmother, meets Valentina, and learns to speak Spanish before school starts in the fall."],"answer":1},{"type":"evidence","q":"Which sentence best shows that Kenji has changed since the start of the summer?","options":["Kenji was about to give up when a young woman's voice came through the speaker, speaking careful English.","He glanced at the screen and then set the phone face down on the seat.","Grandma Hiroko gripped the edge of the table."],"answer":1,"evidence":"He glanced at the screen and then set the phone face down on the seat."},{"type":"inference","q":"Why does Grandma Hiroko buy three copies of the newspaper?","options":["She needs the paper to wrap up the radio for the attic.","She is proud of the story even though she acts embarrassed.","She wants to mail one copy to every operator she knows."],"answer":1}],"grade":5,"words":523,"auto":{"fk":6,"decodable":null,"checks":"pass"}}];
